 Are Probabilities":[126,154],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.4 Conditional Probabilities Are Probabilities#{1}":[128,129],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.4 Conditional Probabilities Are Probabilities#{2}":[130,130],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.4 Conditional Probabilities Are Probabilities#{3}":[131,131],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.4 Conditional Probabilities Are Probabilities#{4}":[132,133],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.4 Conditional Probabilities Are Probabilities#{5}":[134,154],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.5 Independence of Events":[155,185],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.5 Independence of Events#{1}":[157,185],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.6 Coherency of Bayes' Rule":[186,202],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.6 Coherency of Bayes' Rule#{1}":[188,202],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.7 Conditioning as a Problem-Solving Tool":[203,232],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.7 Conditioning as a Problem-Solving Tool#{1}":[205,232],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.8 Pitfalls and Paradoxes":[233,268],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#2.8 Pitfalls and Paradoxes#{1}":[235,268],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways":[269,280],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{1}":[271,271],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{2}":[272,272],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{3}":[273,273],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{4}":[274,274],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{5}":[275,275],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{6}":[276,276],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{7}":[277,278],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Key Takeaways#{8}":[279,280],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Related Documents":[281,296],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Related Documents#{1}":[283,283],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Related Documents#{2}":[284,284],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Related Documents#{3}":[285,286],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Related Documents#{4}":[287,288],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Related Documents#Sources":[289,296],"#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability#Related Documents#Sources#{1}":[291,296]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Introduction to Probability (Main)","target":"<../Introduction to Probability (Main","line":3},{"title":"Infographic","target":"chapter2_infographic.png","line":9,"embedded":true},{"title":"Figure 2.1 — Pebble World intuition for P(A|B): start with A and B, discard the outcomes in B^c, then renormalize the restricted space to total mass 1","target":"chapter2_fig_2.1_pebble_world_conditioning.png","line":43,"embedded":true},{"title":"Figure 2.2 — Frequentist intuition: each row is one repetition; circled rows are those where B occurred, bold rows are those where A occurred. P(A|B) is the fraction of circled rows that are also bold","target":"chapter2_fig_2.2_frequentist_intuition.png","line":49,"embedded":true},{"title":"Figure 2.3 — The partition A_1,…,A_6 slices the sample space; the event B is the union of the disjoint pieces B∩A_i, so P(B) = Σ P(B∩A_i)","target":"chapter2_fig_2.3_lotp_partition.png","line":98,"embedded":true},{"title":"Figure 2.4 — Why P(D|T) is only ~16%: of 10,000 people, 100 are diseased (95 true positives, 5 false negatives) and 9,900 are healthy (495 false positives, 9,405 true negatives). The 495 false positives dwarf the 95 true positives","target":"chapter2_fig_2.4_disease_testing_population.png","line":116,"embedded":true},{"title":"Figure 2.7 — Conditioning as belief-updating: the prior for A, updated by evidence B, becomes the new prior before evidence C is observed. Updating sequentially (B then C) or jointly (B and C) lands on the same posterior","target":"chapter2_fig_2.7_bayesian_updating.png","line":192,"embedded":true},{"title":"Figure 2.5 — Tree diagram of Monty Hall (you pick door 1): the branches sum to a 2/3 chance of getting the car by switching versus 1/3 by staying","target":"chapter2_fig_2.5_monty_hall_tree.png","line":214,"embedded":true},{"title":"Figure 2.6 — Simpson's paradox: white dots are successes, black are failures. Dr. Hibbert (left) is better at both heart and Band-Aid surgery, yet his overall rate is lower because he takes on far more of the difficult heart cases","target":"chapter2_fig_2.6_simpsons_paradox_dots.png","line":265,"embedded":true},{"title":"Chapter 2 — Exercises (2.11)","target":"<chapter2_exercises.md>","line":283},{"title":"Chapter 1 — Probability and Counting","target":"Chapter 1 (Main)","line":284},{"title":"Introduction to Probability (hub)","target":"Introduction to Probability (Main)","line":285}],"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},"smart_blocks:content/Mathematics/Introduction to Probability/Chapter 2 Conditional Probability/Chapter 2 (Main).md#Chapter 2 — Conditional Probability": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":"1dpkg3k","at":1781964148169},"embeddings":{"TaylorAI/bge-micro-v2":{"vec":[-0.09456586,-0.00947397,0.01826973,-0.01457133,-0.02759508,0.04678329,0.07292483,0.0482444,0.05980075,0.02034506,0.03892029,-0.03417025,0.03033993,0.04482457,-0.05540446,0.06583129,-0.02278135,0.02481766,-0.04057131,0.02394939,0.04391682,-0.02964419,0.01825972,-0.02452795,0.08499426,0.06918076,0.01948877,0.02955454,0.0008034,-0.22536677,-0.00646409,0.00816433,-0.03081029,-0.0373671,-0.05506781,-0.0423363,-0.03394601,0.0224868,0.01715739,0.01460186,-0.00259747,0.01083586,-0.02270965,-0.05332495,-0.03193152,-0.08950622,-0.02509172,-0.04142869,-0.08141755,-0.05771674,-0.00631114,0.01750173,-0.00481245,-0.00484006,0.00317687,0.03872667,0.02444639,0.05880741,0.01329307,0.08250221,0.01699801,0.07601324,-0.21135856,0.08479342,0.03117353,0.01251887,-0.04823703,0.00179612,0.01226903,0.05871648,-0.0409883,0.05445361,0.00413565,0.09041546,0.05001837,-0.03044451,-0.00674943,-0.08417223,-0.00089539,-0.02850461,0.00408507,0.06105871,0.00451331,0.